David Morning writes:
>>they give me the merchandise, free and clear.  I may be bound by the law as to
>>what I can do with the merchandise; for example, the copyright law states that
>>I can't run off copies of the book for my friends.  (In the case of software
>>packages, the law explicitly allows one archival backup copy as "fair use",
>>something that I don't believe has changed under the Berne convention.)
>
>Only partly correct. Berne specifies quite clearly what is meant by 'fair use'
>namely, you may copy *portions* of the original, not the entire work.
>How big a portion depends on intended usage - personal, commercial and 
>educational.

Making an archival copy in the United States has nothing to do with
fair use.  It is *EXPLICITLY* allowed by United States copyright law:

+============================================================+
| s 117. Limitations on exclusive rights:  Computer programs |
|                                                            |
| Notwithstanding the provisions of section 106, it is not   |
| an infringement for the owner of a copy of a computer      |
| program to make or authorize the making of another copy or |
| adaptation of that computer program provided:              |
|                                                            |
| (1) that such a new copy or adaptation is created as an    |
| essential step in the utilization of the computer program  |
| in conjunction with a machine and that it is used in no    |
| other manner, or                                           |
|                                                            |
| (2) that such new copy or adaptation is for archival       |
| purposes only and that all archival copies are destroyed   |
| in the event that continued possession of the computer     |
| program should cease to be rightful.                       |
|                                                            |
| Any exact copies prepared in accordance with the           |
| provisions of this section may be leased, sold, or         |
| otherwise transferred, along with the copy from which such |
| copies were prepared, only as part of the lease, sale, or  |
| other transfer of all rights in the program.  Adaptations  |
| so prepared may be transferred only with the authorization |
| of the copyright owner.                                    |
+============================================================+

>As to what you refer to as 'shrinkwrap licenses', I think you'll find that
>anyone who tries to challenge them these days will simply have Berne laws
>thrown back at them in court and come a cropper.

Anyone who tries to enforce a shrinkwrap license in the U.S. is likely to
have UCC 2-207 thrown at them.  This is not speculation.  This is Step-Saver
Data Systems, Inc., v. Wyse Technology and Software Link, Inc., 939 F.2d 91
(3d cir. 1991).

There are also numerous law review articles that have analyzed these licenses,
and I've not found any that think that they will be enforceable in any other
jurisdiction.

Modern contract law tends to throw out things that aren't bargained for.
The reason for this is simple.  Consider the following scenerio:

	You order some item by filling out one of your standard purchase
	order forms, with find print on the back, of course, and send it
	to the seller.  Under common law, this would be an offer.

	Seller ships the item, and it shows up on your loading dock.
	Seller's truck driver tells your shipping clerk to sign for
	the thing.  The form your clerk is asked to sign contains a
	bunch of fine print on the back that doesn't match the fine
	print on your purchase order.  Under common law, because the
	terms did not match *exactly*, this was treated as a rejection
	of your offer, and the offering of a counter-offer from the seller.

	Your clerk signs, thereby accepting the counter-offer and all the
	seller's terms.

Well, you can predict what this would lead to.

	Every time something arrives at the loading dock, you will have
	to have your corporate counsel come down and read the find print
	on the back of the seller's form.  If he doesn't like something,
	he can cross it out and then sign. But now if the delivery person
	leaves the item, it's probably an acceptance of your counter-counter-
	offer.  So the seller is going to have to send a lawyer out on all
	their deliveries...  give me a break!

This is called "the battle of the forms," and caused many problems.

The UCC deals with this as follows:

	Section 2-207.  Additional Terms in Acceptance or Confirmation

	(1) A definite and seasonable expression of acceptance or a written
	confirmation which is sent within a reasonable time operates as an
	acceptance even though it states terms additional or different from
	those offrered or agreed upon, unless acceptance is expressly made
	conditional on assent to the additional or different terms.

	(2) The additional terms are to be construed as proposals for additions
	to the contract.  Between merchants such terms become part of the
	contract unless:

		(a) the offer expressly limits acceptance to the terms of the
		offer;

		(b) they materially alter it; or

		(c) notification of objection to them has already been given
		or is given within a reasonable time after notice of them
		is received.

	(3) Conduct by both parties which recognizes the existence of a
	contract is sufficient to establish a contract for sale although
	the writings of the parties do not otherwise establish a contract.
	In such case the terms of the particular contract consist of
	those terms on which the writings of the parties agree, together
	with any supplementary terms incorporated under any other
	provisions of this Act.

So, what happens when you call up a company and order a program?  First of
all, when they ship it to you, that is an acceptance under 2-207(1).  Is it
expressly conditional on you accepting the terms of the license in the box?
No.  Courts have construed this very narrowly.  They would have to refuse to
ship until you accepted the license.  So we have an acceptance.

Now take a look at 2-207(2).  The license proposes additional or different
terms or both, depending on exactly what it says.  If this were a sale
between merchants, they would become part of the contract unless one of
the exceptions were met.  However, you aren't a merchant in this example,
so they don't become oart if the contract.  They merely stay as proposals
for additions to the contract.

If you were a merchant, the seller still has a problem.  2-207(2)(b) tends
to nail the kind of terms that sellers like to try.

Anyway, you tend to end up in 2-207(3), and you get all the usual stuff that
goes with the sale of goods, such as warranties.

